<h3 id="31-bytes-delimiters-and-yaml">3.1 Bytes, delimiters and YAML</h3>
<p>Readers and writers MUST apply these checks to original bytes before parsing:</p>
<ul>
<li>UTF-8 decoding is fatal: reject malformed, truncated, overlong or surrogate encodings and code points above U+10FFFF. Do not replace bad bytes with U+FFFD. An intentionally encoded U+FFFD is an ordinary scalar value.</li>
<li>Accept zero or one initial UTF-8 BOM (<code>EF BB BF</code>); remove it for delimiter recognition. A second BOM or U+FEFF elsewhere in the file is invalid.</li>
<li>The opening line is exactly <code>---</code> followed by LF or CRLF. The next line exactly <code>---</code> closes the frontmatter, followed by LF, CRLF or end of file. Leading spaces, trailing spaces, bare CR and other line endings are invalid delimiters. Mixed LF/CRLF is allowed; a bare CR anywhere in the file is invalid.</li>
<li>Frontmatter size is <strong>at most 20 KiB = 20,480 bytes</strong>: start immediately after the opening line’s LF and stop immediately before the first hyphen of the closing delimiter. All internal line endings, including the last one before the closing delimiter, count, and so do YAML comments such as the compiled header (§ 10). Delimiter lines and the initial BOM do not count.</li>
<li>Body size is <strong>at most 4 KiB = 4,096 bytes</strong>: all bytes after the closing delimiter line’s line ending (zero if it ends at EOF). A <code>HUMANS.md</code> has zero body bytes, including whitespace. Other <code>.md</code> profile files may have a human-facing body within the bound. Consumers MUST NOT serve any body.</li>
<li>Bound the complete read before parsing: at most <strong>24,589 bytes</strong> (20,480 + 4,096 + two 5-byte CRLF delimiter lines + a 3-byte BOM). A bounded read may request one extra byte only to detect overflow. Check the component limits too. Count raw bytes before CRLF normalization, decoding normalization or YAML processing.</li>
</ul>
<p>The frontmatter is exactly one YAML 1.2 core-schema document with a mapping root. Validators and consumers MUST reject anchors, aliases, explicit tags, directives, document-end markers, duplicate mapping keys (including equivalent decoded spellings), non-string mapping keys and non-JSON values. Every mapping is processed as data, never as object prototypes or executable constructors. The first exact closing delimiter always terminates frontmatter; later text is body, never a second profile. A document marker encountered as YAML syntax within the extracted frontmatter is invalid. Reject unpaired surrogates in decoded YAML strings and keys, including escapes in unknown fields, before filtering.</p>
<h3 id="32-identity-and-common-fields">3.2 Identity and common fields</h3>
<p>All fields are optional except these four: <code>humans</code>, <code>id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>updated</code>. Absence and an empty optional list both mean no preference. An empty list emits no vote or artificial value; empty projected arrays and blocks are omitted except mandatory <code>withheld</code> / <code>conflicts</code> arrays. The special conservative rule for group <code>knows_well</code> is in § 9.</p>
