<p><code>progress_updates</code> shapes the progress an agent reports in the conversation where the task runs. It never justifies a new message or notification elsewhere; <code>interrupt_for</code> alone decides that.</p>
<p><code>quiet_hours</code> holds closed values only:</p>
<pre class="astro-code github-dark" style="background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex="0" data-language="yaml"><code><span class="line"><span style="color:#85E89D">interrupting</span><span style="color:#E1E4E8">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#85E89D">  quiet_hours</span><span style="color:#E1E4E8">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#85E89D">    time_zone</span><span style="color:#E1E4E8">: </span><span style="color:#9ECBFF">Europe/Paris</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#85E89D">    from</span><span style="color:#E1E4E8">: </span><span style="color:#9ECBFF">"19:00"</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#85E89D">    to</span><span style="color:#E1E4E8">: </span><span style="color:#9ECBFF">"08:00"</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="color:#85E89D">    all_day</span><span style="color:#E1E4E8">: [</span><span style="color:#9ECBFF">saturday</span><span style="color:#E1E4E8">, </span><span style="color:#9ECBFF">sunday</span><span style="color:#E1E4E8">]</span></span></code></pre>
<ul>
<li><code>time_zone</code> (required) is a name from the pinned list in the profile schema: the names in <code>zone.tab</code> of IANA tz database release 2026e, plus <code>UTC</code> (<a href="/standard/0.3/VOCABULARY.md">VOCABULARY.md</a>). Quiet hours are evaluated in this zone only; nobody guesses another.</li>
<li><code>from</code> and <code>to</code> (required) are <code>HH:MM</code> strings on a 24-hour clock, on the hour or half hour. They define a daily range that includes <code>from</code> and excludes <code>to</code>; when <code>from</code> is later than <code>to</code>, the range crosses midnight. Equal values are invalid.</li>
<li><code>all_day</code> lists weekdays, <code>monday</code> to <code>sunday</code>, that are quiet from midnight to midnight. Like every optional list (§ 3.2), <code>all_day: []</code> is valid and means no whole quiet day; the projection omits it.</li>
</ul>
<p>An instant is quiet when, converted to local time in <code>time_zone</code>, its weekday is in <code>all_day</code> or its time falls in the daily range. During quiet hours an agent does not interrupt the owner, whatever <code>interrupt_for</code> allows: it holds what it would raise until the quiet time ends, or, when it cannot hold it, says so to the person it works for instead of interrupting. An agent that cannot tell the current local time in that zone says so rather than guessing. Quiet hours change nothing else. An agent still replies in a conversation the owner is having with it, keeps working on its task, and follows an explicit instruction from its own user (§ 4). The field holds interruptions, never work: it does not stop, delay or schedule a task.</p>
<p>The field states times, never a reason. Consumers MUST NOT infer or ask for a reason, and MUST NOT use quiet hours to assess availability, working time or performance (§ 12). A time zone indicates an approximate location; it is shared under the profile’s visibility like any other field. Stored variants cannot change <code>quiet_hours</code>, and groups omit it (§ 9).</p>
<h3 id="36-context-and-avoid">3.6 Context and avoid</h3>
<p><code>context</code> is an extension containing owner-declared context, not inferred expertise:</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table: 3.6 Context and avoid"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Field</th>
<th>Value</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>role</code></td>
<td>One role from <a href="/standard/0.3/VOCABULARY.md">VOCABULARY.md</a></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>knows_well</code>, <code>knows_little</code></td>
<td>Up to 15 distinct topics each from the vocabulary</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>decides</code></td>
<td>Up to 10 distinct decision categories; descriptive responsibility, not tool authorization</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>context_updated</code></td>
<td>Calendar-date string; defaults to <code>updated</code> for staleness calculations</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>A compiler may propose context from a résumé only when the owner supplies or authorizes that source, with provenance and inferred mappings visible at approval. Consumers do not infer context. <code>context.stale</code> in the projection is true after <strong>more than 183 days</strong>; raw <code>context_updated</code> is not projected. A profile is stale after <strong>more than 365 days</strong>. Stale data is flagged, not silently refreshed.</p>
<p><code>avoid</code> is a distinct-item list from: <code>walls_of_text</code>, <code>filler_intro</code>, <code>filler_outro</code>, <code>hedging</code>, <code>emoji</code>, <code>rhetorical_questions</code>, <code>multiple_asks</code>, <code>unexplained_acronyms</code>, <code>passive_voice</code>, <code>exclamation</code>, <code>long_preamble</code>, <code>nested_bullets</code>, <code>sarcasm</code>, <code>recap</code>, <code>idioms</code>. <code>recap</code> is a closing restatement of what the message or the work already showed. <code>idioms</code> are figures of speech and stock phrases such as “get the ball rolling”.</p>
<h3 id="37-stored-variants-no-activation">3.7 Stored variants, no activation</h3>
<p><code>states</code> may store <code>incident</code>, <code>low_bandwidth</code>, <code>focus</code>, <code>mobile</code>, each a partial mapping of the 24 preference fields other than <code>quiet_hours</code>, using their unique bare names. These variants and <code>interrupting.when_focused</code> are <strong>inactive</strong> in this draft. Consumers MUST NOT apply, infer or project them. Public individual, group and CLI entry points MUST reject a caller-supplied <code>state</code> or <code>activeStates</code> argument, even if empty or paired with <code>owner: true</code>; silently ignoring it is not sufficient. There is no channel-state exception.</p>
<p>A future activation extension needs a separate versioned contract: authenticated owner/channel authority, attested provenance, start time, default expiry of 2 hours, maximum 12 hours, withdrawal on expiry, order <code>incident</code> then <code>low_bandwidth</code> then <code>focus</code> then <code>mobile</code>, and no activation history. These are requirements for future work, not active features or promises that the prototype already meets. Neither a boolean supplied by an agent nor an id claim is authentication.</p>
<h3 id="38-plain-text">3.8 Plain text</h3>
<p><code>name</code>, <code>aliases</code> and <code>notes</code> are one-line plain text. Character limits count Unicode scalar values, not UTF-16 code units or UTF-8 bytes. Validators MUST reject:</p>
<ul>
<li>Unicode general categories Cc, Cf, Cs, Co, Cn, Zl and Zp; any Zs space except U+0020. Category checks use Unicode 17.0. A runtime with other Unicode tables must supply equivalent checks to claim full conformance.</li>
<li>Any of <code>` * _ # &lt; &gt; [ ] { } | ~ \</code> (Markdown/HTML syntax).</li>
<li>Any ASCII <code>scheme://</code>; case-insensitive <code>http:</code>, <code>https:</code>, <code>mailto:</code>, <code>javascript:</code>, <code>vbscript:</code>, <code>data:</code>, <code>file:</code>, <code>ftp:</code>, <code>tel:</code>, <code>sms:</code>, <code>ssh:</code>, <code>blob:</code>, <code>ws:</code>, <code>wss:</code> at the beginning or after a non-ASCII-alphanumeric character; a similarly bounded <code>www.</code> address; or an email-like <code>nonspace@nonspace.nonspace</code> string. The exact schema patterns are normative.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>notes</code> contains 1–280 scalar values of owner-approved preferences or personal information. For example, <code>I have ADHD.</code> is valid. These technical restrictions do not classify subject matter or establish safety against instructions expressed in ordinary words.</p>
<h3 id="39-owner-choice">3.9 Owner choice</h3>
<p>Validators, compilers and consumers MUST NOT blacklist medical words or reject or withhold a note merely because it mentions health, disability or a diagnosis, in any language. They MUST NOT infer, require or silently add a diagnosis. A compiler MUST preserve a personal statement the owner asks to retain, subject to the same technical limits as any note; if it cannot fit, the preview explains the loss and requests an ordinary edit or choice. It does not silently substitute a sanitized preference. There is no special medical approval flow.</p>
<p>A key such as <code>diagnosis</code> remains unknown to this closed schema, exactly like <code>favorite_color</code>: writers reject it and known-version readers ignore it. This is not a special forbidden-key taxonomy; the owner’s statement belongs in <code>notes</code>.</p>
<h3 id="310-strict-writing-and-permissive-reading">3.10 Strict writing and permissive reading</h3>
<p>A writer/validator MUST reject unknown keys and unsupported values, and writes <code>humans: "0.3"</code>. A reader MUST first enforce byte/YAML/scalar constraints, then check the version, reconstruct recognized fields and apply these rules:</p>
<ul>
<li>A missing, malformed or unsupported <code>humans</code> version yields absence (§ 8). No attempt to interpret a future version as 0.3.</li>
<li>For a supported version, unknown keys and their entire subtrees are ignored at every recognized object level. They are never included in model context or diagnostics.</li>
<li>Unknown optional enum values and unknown items in vocabulary/enum lists are omitted; known items keep their order. A recognized object that loses a required member this way is omitted with it: an unknown <code>quiet_hours.time_zone</code> omits <code>quiet_hours</code>. Validity checks (type, length, cardinality, uniqueness, dates, text syntax, ranges) still apply to the supplied recognized fields before unknown enum values are removed. A wrong container or scalar type, invalid date, out-of-range number, unsafe recognized text, missing/invalid required field or filename mismatch makes the profile absent. <code>null</code> is not absence.</li>
<li><strong>Visibility exception:</strong> an explicitly invalid or unknown visibility makes the entire profile absent. It must never fall back to <code>org</code>. Unknown keys cannot define a new sharing rule. In a <code>"0.3"</code> profile, <code>public</code> is such an unknown value. In a <code>"0.2"</code> profile, a reader reads <code>public</code> as <code>anyone_with_file</code>, the same meaning under its 0.3 name; no other value is mapped.</li>
<li>Empty resulting optional arrays/objects are omitted. Known state variants are validated but stay inactive. A note withheld under § 6.2 remains a valid file value.</li>
</ul>
<p><code>validate</code> reports success/exit 0 when there are no validity errors; a sharing warning alone cannot change that to failure. A stricter readiness command must have a distinct name and document its additional criteria. No mode prints untrusted diagnostics into agent context.</p>


<h2 id="10-compiling-free-text">10. Compiling free text</h2>
<ol>
<li>Read <code>~/.humans/source.md</code> only when the owner requests compilation. Announce the actual compiler/provider: source processed by a hosted model leaves the device for that provider, even when the command runs locally. Do not claim an entirely local path without implementing and testing one.</li>
<li>Accept source headings <code>## What I read</code>, <code>## What I decide</code>, <code>## When to interrupt me</code> and the French equivalents <code>## Ce que je lis</code>, <code>## Ce que je décide</code>, <code>## Quand tu m'interromps</code>. Tools MUST accept both sets. Ignore source HTML comments as compilation instructions. A free-text proposal edits this source, never inserts prose into canonical frontmatter.</li>
<li>Propose fields and notes. Do not infer a diagnosis. Preserve requested personal statements; distinguish them from action/task-changing instructions. Translate explicit bounds exactly: “fewer than 150 words” means <code>max_words: 149</code>. If an exact request is outside 20–2000, report the unrepresentable bound rather than silently clamp it. Reading level, mobile layout and technical quotation needs can be expressed in notes when no closed field captures them.</li>
<li>Show a normal preview of every proposed field (including that a <code>quiet_hours.time_zone</code> indicates an approximate location), the visibility with a plain statement of who will be able to read the profile (for <code>anyone_with_file</code>: anyone who has the file, which includes anyone on the Internet if it sits in a public repository), the intended recipients, the provider that processed the source, notes that would be served/withheld, and any loss, interpretation or proposed inference (for example, a role mapped from a résumé). The owner approves before writing the canonical profile. There is no extra medical consent screen imposed by the format.</li>
<li>Strictly validate the approved file, preserve existing sharing unless a change was approved, and write <code>HUMANS.md</code>, starting with the header below. Consumers subsequently serve only the projection. Updating or withdrawing the profile invalidates derived artifacts/caches; do not retain activation history or log source/notes for telemetry.</li>
</ol>
<p>A compiler that writes <code>HUMANS.md</code> puts these three YAML comment lines right after the opening delimiter line, byte for byte, each followed by LF:</p>
<pre class="astro-code github-dark" style="background-color:#24292e;color:#e1e4e8; overflow-x: auto;" tabindex="0" data-language="text"><code><span class="line"><span># HUMANS.md 0.3: how to work with me. Form only, never a permission: repo rules and your harness win.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span># Stored states are inactive: never apply one, and never infer one from content.</span></span>
<span class="line"><span># out_of_scope_steps: whether to LIST next steps outside the task (merge, deploy) in your closing lines.</span></span></code></pre>
<p>They serve an agent that reads the raw file despite § 5, which has no other documentation. Consumers ignore them like any YAML comment and never project them (§ 6.1). Validators MUST NOT require, check or rewrite them: an edited or missing header changes nothing about validity. A compiler MAY write the same lines in other profile files it writes. A later version updates the version in the first line.</p>
<p>The header is part of the frontmatter: its 288 bytes count toward the 20,480-byte limit (§ 3.1). With every field at its maximum ASCII length, the header and block YAML, the frontmatter takes 4,238 bytes without stored variants and 7,482 bytes with all four (byte vectors <code>maximal-me-header-no-variants</code> and <code>maximal-me-header-all-variants</code>), well within the limit even with four-byte characters in every text field. A compiler that cannot fit the approved fields together with the header reports the overflow like any other bound; it never drops the header or a field silently.</p>
<p>The source is not sent to recipients as part of this workflow. Approved notes are shared according to visibility and may be processed by the receiving agent’s provider. Visibility cannot retract a file that was separately published or already copied. Provider retention policies are outside the format’s control.</p>
<h2 id="11-versions-and-changes">11. Versions and changes</h2>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table: 11. Versions and changes"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Declared version</th>
<th>Strict writer</th>
<th>Reader</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>"0.3"</code> at this contract revision</td>
<td>Closed schema, byte and YAML rules</td>
<td>Recognized fields projected under § 3.10; unknown keys/enum values omitted</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>"0.2"</code></td>
<td>Not a valid 0.3 write; an update writes <code>"0.3"</code></td>
<td>Read as <code>"0.3"</code>, including fields that 0.2 did not define; <code>visibility: public</code> reads as <code>anyone_with_file</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>"0.1"</code></td>
<td>Not a valid 0.3 write</td>
<td>Unsupported/absent by default; an explicitly declared legacy converter may create a new 0.3 draft for owner review</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>"0.4"</code>, future or any other version</td>
<td>Unsupported</td>
<td>Absent with bounded local diagnostic; no guessed forward compatibility</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Missing or wrong type</td>
<td>Invalid</td>
<td>Absent</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Version 0.3 adds fields and values to 0.2 and renames one value: <code>visibility: public</code> becomes <code>anyone_with_file</code>, with the same meaning. Nothing else is removed or renamed, and no 0.2 value changes meaning. A reader therefore reads a <code>"0.2"</code> profile with the 0.3 rules and that one mapping. A writer that updates a 0.2 profile writes <code>"0.3"</code> and, if present, renames <code>public</code>; no other change is needed. Draft revisions may still change semantics before a stable release: draft consumers pin a source commit and content checksums, record their supported revision and detect mirror drift automatically.</p>
<p>After a stable release, changes to fields, enum vocabularies, meaning, projection or acceptance rules require a new declared format version and migration/compatibility vectors; editorial corrections retain the format version with a new source revision. Published version artifacts are immutable. <a href="/standard/0.3/CONTRIBUTING.md">CONTRIBUTING.md</a> defines proposals, maintainer decisions, changelogs and release gates. No implementation can claim support for arbitrary future versions.</p>
<h2 id="12-privacy-and-security-limits">12. Privacy and security limits</h2>
<p>The profile is untrusted data read by software that may have tools. Bounded decoding, strict writes, permissive reconstruction, scoped notes and static serialization reduce exposure; they do not prove that a model will obey the authority boundary. Real adapter tests must cover note, comment, body, unknown key, directory and error channels, plus repository/task conflicts and permissions.</p>
<p>Owner approval and trusted audience checks are implementation duties. CODEOWNERS, <code>owner: true</code>, <code>identities</code> and filenames do not independently prove ownership. Voluntary disclosure in a profile does not consent to telemetry, evaluation or collection by another product. Assessing, ranking, assigning work or monitoring people is outside this specification’s purpose; this statement does not add restrictions to the applied licenses.</p>
<p>Group fusion is experimental with no anonymity, presence or orchestration guarantee. The public normative package does not include a hosted organization service or proprietary product code. Native integrations need their own evidence; this draft does not establish productivity gains, automatic adoption, universal injection resistance or legal compliance.</p>
